<section class="page-hero compact"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs"><a href="../index.html">Home</a> / <a href="index.html">Tools</a> / <span>Sunrise &amp; sunset</span></nav><h1>Sunrise &amp; sunset calculator</h1><p class="lede">Enter a latitude, longitude and date — or use your location.</p></div></section>
<section class="wrap" id="tool" data-tool="sun">
  <div class="row2"><div class="field"><label for="lat">Latitude</label><input id="lat" type="number" step="any" value="45.5019" min="-90" max="90"></div><div class="field"><label for="lon">Longitude</label><input id="lon" type="number" step="any" value="-73.5674" min="-180" max="180"></div></div>
  <div class="row2"><div class="field"><label for="date">Date</label><input id="date" type="date"></div><div class="field"><label>&nbsp;</label><button class="btn btn-ghost btn-block" id="me" type="button" style="margin:0">Use my location</button></div></div>
  <div class="tool-out" aria-live="polite"></div>
</section>
<section class="wrap prose"><h2>Why day length changes</h2><p>Earth's axis is tilted about 23.4° relative to its orbit. Around the June solstice the Northern Hemisphere leans toward the Sun, so days there are long and the Sun climbs high; in December the situation reverses. At the equinoxes (around 20 March and 22 September) day and night are close to 12 hours everywhere. Above the Arctic Circle (66.6° N) the Sun can stay up all day in summer — the midnight sun — and not rise at all in winter.</p><p><b>Civil twilight</b> is the period when the Sun is less than 6° below the horizon: there's enough light for most outdoor activities without artificial lighting. Photographers chase the "golden hour" just after sunrise and before sunset, when low sunlight is warm and soft.</p></section>
